and leads Guckian by 20 or more points in every recent poll, while all seven raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic and markets price a Democratic win near 97 percent. Rhode Island's Democratic lean and a fragmented opposition field make an upset unlikely.", "watch": "If Guckian withdraws and anti-Democratic voters consolidate behind Ken Block, the race could tighten, though Foulkes still led the actual field in the latest polling.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governor race is an open-seat contest after Dan McKee lost the Democratic primary to Helena Foulkes, a rare primary defeat driven partly by backlash over a stalled bridge project. Foulkes now faces Republican Aaron Guckian and independents Ken Block, CD Reynolds, and Jasjit Gotra. Recent polls of likely voters show Foulkes leading Guckian by roughly 22 to 27 points, with the Republican stuck in the high teens.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the contest Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price a Democratic win around 97 percent. The state's Democratic lean and a fragmented opposition field reinforce that picture. Block is pushing a two-way race and points to a campaign poll showing him ahead of Foulkes head-to-head, but Guckian says he is staying in, keeping the field split and the Democratic lead intact.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 19.0}]}